What happens when an application start().

->Main Method Execution and SpringApplication.run(): The process begins with the execution of the main method in the entry point class, typically annotated with @SpringBootApplication. This method internally invokes SpringApplication.run(), which is the core of the Spring Boot bootstrapping process.
->Environment Setup and Banner Display: The run() method prepares the application environment, considering profiles (e.g., dev, prod) and configuration files like application.properties or application.yml. It also displays the application banner.
->Application Context Creation and Refresh: Spring Boot identifies the application type (e.g., web application if spring-boot-starter-web is present) and creates the ApplicationContext object, which serves as the Spring IoC container. This context is then refreshed, triggering various initialization phases.
->Component Scanning and Bean Instantiation: Spring Boot automatically scans the classpath for components annotated with @Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller, etc., and registers them as beans within the ApplicationContext. It then instantiates and wires these beans, resolving dependencies through dependency injection.
->Auto-Configuration: A key feature of Spring Boot, auto-configuration dynamically configures the application based on the dependencies present in the classpath. For instance, if spring-boot-starter-web is included, Spring Boot automatically configures an embedded web server (like Tomcat) and sets up request handling. Similarly, adding spring-boot-starter-data-jpa triggers the configuration of database connections and JPA repositories. 
Embedded Server Startup (for web applications): If it's a web application, Spring Boot starts an embedded servlet container (e.g., Tomcat, Jetty, Undertow) and registers the DispatcherServlet to handle incoming HTTP requests.
Application Runners Execution: Optional CommandLineRunner and ApplicationRunner beans, if defined, are executed at this stage, allowing for custom logic to be performed right after the application context is fully initialized.
Application Ready: Once all these steps are complete, the application is considered "ready" and is capable of handling requests or performing its intended tasks. Spring Boot publishes ApplicationReadyEvent at this point, which can be listened to for post-startup operations.

