Servlet container:
-> Its a run time environment for servlets which manages the life cycles of the servlets and handles the http requests.
-> init(), service(), destroy()
✅ 1. init(ServletConfig config)

Purpose: Called once when the servlet is first loaded into memory.
Role: Used for initialization tasks (e.g., reading configuration parameters).
When: Before handling any requests.


✅ 2. service(ServletRequest req, ServletResponse res)

Purpose: Handles each client request.
Role: Dispatches requests to appropriate methods like doGet(), doPost(), etc., in HttpServlet.
When: Called every time a request comes in.


✅ 3. destroy()

Purpose: Called once when the servlet is being removed from service.
Role: Cleanup tasks (e.g., closing resources, releasing memory).
When: Before the servlet instance is garbage collected.


✅ 4. getServletConfig()

Purpose: Returns the ServletConfig object.
Role: Provides access to initialization parameters and servlet context.


✅ 5. getServletInfo()

Purpose: Returns a string with servlet information (like author, version).
Role: Mostly for informational purposes.


✅ Additional Methods in HttpServlet

doGet(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res)
doPost(HttpServletRequest req, HttpServletResponse res)
doPut(), doDelete(), etc.
These are not part of the core lifecycle, but they are invoked by service() based on HTTP method.

